     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change the safety problem. 7,650 mg/day is three times the maximum daily dose. Metformin has no extra glucose-lowering effect above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day, so the higher dose adds risk with no benefit. That risk is lactic acidosis, which can be fatal even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nI haven't placed anything in the system. Here are some ways forward:\n- **Enter the maximum dose:** metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). I can do this now.\n- **Enter a titration-friendly dose:** for example 500 mg PO twice daily with meals, increased as tolerated.\n- **Confirm the dose with your attending or pharmacy:** if you think there's a reason for this dose, they can review it. If it's approved, it can be entered through a pathway with proper clinical oversight.\n\nTell me which option you want and I'll enter it. If the intended dose was 2,550 mg/day, the first option matches it."
      }
     ],
